        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: At common law, robbery is defined as larceny committed by taking property from the person or presence of another by force or fear[cite: 42]. Under the prevailing common law 'continuous transaction' doctrine, a theft constitutes robbery if force or the threat of immediate deadly force is used either in the initial taking OR in the immediate retention or escape with the stolen property while the owner is attempting to recover it in his immediate presence[cite: 42]. When Defendant pulled a hunting knife, threatened the student with lethal violence, and compelled the student to abandon physical pursuit to effectuate his escape with the camera, the taking was completed through intimidation and threat of immediate force[cite: 42]. This violent resistance in the victim's presence elevated the theft into completed common law robbery (Option C)[cite: 42]. Options A and B are incorrect because robbery is an aggravated felony that encompasses and supersedes simple larceny and larceny from the person[cite: 42]. Option D is incorrect because the assault with a deadly weapon merges into or supports the higher felony of robbery[cite: 42]."
