        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: In tort actions for deceit (intentional misrepresentation), jurisdictions split on the measure of compensatory damages[cite: 42]. The minority 'out-of-pocket' rule awards the difference between the purchase price paid and the actual market value of the property received ($180,000 - $3,000 = $177,000)[cite: 42]. The majority 'benefit-of-the-bargain' rule awards the difference between the fair market value of the property as represented and the actual fair market value received ($320,000 - $3,000 = $317,000)[cite: 42]. Because the jurisdiction follows the majority rule, the collector is entitled to $317,000 (Option B)[cite: 42]. Option A reflects the out-of-pocket rule[cite: 42]. Option C reflects rescissionary restitution rather than tort damages[cite: 42]. Option D reflects the difference between represented value and purchase price, which is not the standard tort formula[cite: 42]."
