        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: Under the common law of contracts (Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 84 and § 150), a party who is the beneficiary of an express condition precedent (such as a time-of-the-essence closing date) may waive that condition by words or conduct[cite: 42]. While an executory waiver made without consideration can ordinarily be retracted before the condition is due upon reasonable notice, a waiver CANNOT be retracted if the other party has materially and detrimentally changed his position in reliance on the waiver[cite: 42]. The investor foreseeably and detrimentally changed position by letting his bridge financing lapse in reliance on the landlord's oral assent to October 10[cite: 42]. The landlord is equitably estopped from asserting the October 1 deadline, and the investor is entitled to specific performance (Option C)[cite: 42]. Options A and B are incorrect because detrimental reliance makes an oral waiver of a closing date binding and irrevocable without a signed writing or fresh consideration[cite: 42]. Option D is incorrect because time-of-the-essence clauses are fully valid in commercial real estate contracts[cite: 42]."
