        fp: "A homeowner engaged an excavation contractor to dig a basement extension adjacent to his property line[cite: 42]. The contractor brought a heavy hydraulic backhoe onto the property[cite: 42]. While operating the backhoe within four feet of the property boundary, the contractor failed to install standard timber trench shoring or metal trench boxes, in direct violation of municipal excavation safety ordinances[cite: 42]. The unreinforced earthen embankment collapsed, removing the lateral support of the neighbor's adjoining land[cite: 42]. The neighbor's natural soil subsided, creating a massive six-foot sinkhole that cracked the foundation of the neighbor's detached brick garage[cite: 42]. In the neighbor's action against the excavation contractor, uncontroverted geological expert testimony established that the neighbor's land would have subsided from the excavation even in its natural, unimproved state without the weight of the garage[cite: 42].",
